-- WHO IS A PERSON UNDER DISABILITY.  Rule 1 defines the terms of this Order; the other functions here take "the person is a person under
-- disability" as a plain yes/no input, which the seat answers from `o 44 r 1 a person is a person under disability`.
-- "Despite anything in Order 6, Rule 1(2)" (Rule 10(1)) is a flat override (S-L2): no Order 6 input is threaded.

§ `Order 44 -- Disability`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Order 44 Rule 1 -- definitions`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Rule 1: "In this Order, unless the context otherwise requires — 'Act' means the Mental Capacity Act; 'person lacking capacity' means ...; 'person under disability' means, subject to Order 4, Rule 1 — (a) a person who is a minor; or (b) a person lacking capacity."
-- The lead-in "unless the context otherwise requires" is a condition introduced with "unless": when the context requires otherwise the definition gives no meaning (JUST FALSE).
-- src:5974 | 1. In this Order, unless the context otherwise requires — “Act” means the Mental Capacity Act;
@ref O 44 r 1 (src:5974)
GIVEN `the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the statute is the Mental Capacity Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 44 r 1 a statute is the Act` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the context otherwise requires`
           THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the context otherwise requires"
           IF `the statute is the Mental Capacity Act`
           THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“Act” means the Mental Capacity Act"

-- src:5975 | “person lacking capacity” means a person who lacks capacity within the meaning of the Act in relation to matters concerning his or her property and affairs;
@ref O 44 r 1 (src:5975)
GIVEN `the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person lacks capacity within the meaning of the Act in relation to matters concerning the person's property and affairs` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 44 r 1 a person is a person lacking capacity` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the context otherwise requires`
           THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the context otherwise requires"
           IF `the person lacks capacity within the meaning of the Act in relation to matters concerning the person's property and affairs`
           THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“person lacking capacity” means a person who lacks capacity within the meaning of the Act in relation to matters concerning his or her property and affairs"

-- "Subject to Order 4, Rule 1": what Order 4, Rule 1 says belongs to part roc1; whether it takes the person out of the meaning is an input.
-- FORK F44.3 | "a person who is a minor", "subject to Order 4, Rule 1": Order 4, Rule 1(a) lets "a person who is 18 years of age or older but below 21 years of age and where section 36 of the Civil Law Act applies" be a party in his or her own name; whether that takes such a person out of "minor" here is the caller's fact `o 4 r 1 Order 4, Rule 1 provides otherwise for the person`, and when it is TRUE the answer is NOTHING | such a person is not a person under disability (JUST FALSE), or is still a minor (JUST TRUE)
-- src:5976 | “person under disability” means, subject to Order 4, Rule 1 — ( a )
-- src:5977 | a person who is a minor; or
-- src:5978 | ( b )
-- src:5979 | a person lacking capacity.
@ref O 44 r 1 (src:5976-5979)
GIVEN `the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN
      `o 4 r 1 Order 4, Rule 1 provides otherwise for the person` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a minor` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is a person lacking capacity` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 44 r 1 a person is a person under disability` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the context otherwise requires`
           THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the context otherwise requires"
           IF `o 4 r 1 Order 4, Rule 1 provides otherwise for the person`
           THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "subject to Order 4, Rule 1" -- what Order 4, Rule 1 provides is in another part
           IF `the person is a minor` OR `the person is a person lacking capacity`
           THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“person under disability” means ... (a) a person who is a minor; or (b) a person lacking capacity"

