-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Order 44 Rule 9 -- compromise by a person under disability`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Rule 9: "Where in any proceedings money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability, no settlement, compromise or payment and no acceptance of money paid into Court, whenever entered into or made, is, so far
-- as it relates to that person's claim, valid without the approval of the Court."
-- Whether approval is REQUIRED: the rule speaks "Where in any proceedings money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability", so where no money is claimed (or the act is not a settlement, compromise,
-- payment or acceptance of money paid into Court) approval is not required (a failed "where", JUST FALSE).
-- src:6036 | 9. Where in any proceedings money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability, no settlement, compromise or payment and no acceptance of money paid into Court, whenever entered into or made, is, so far as it relates to that person’s claim, valid without the approval of the Court.
@ref O 44 r 9 (src:6036)
GIVEN `money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act is a settlement, compromise or payment, or an acceptance of money paid into Court, so far as it relates to that person's claim` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 44 r 9 the approval of the Court is required` MEANS
    IF `money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability` AND `the act is a settlement, compromise or payment, or an acceptance of money paid into Court, so far as it relates to that person's claim`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where in any proceedings money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability, no settlement, compromise or payment and no acceptance of money paid into Court" (the condition is not met)

-- FORK QA | the Court has approved: NOTHING (a lifted bar says nothing about validity) | JUST TRUE (the exception makes it valid)
-- src:6036 | 9. Where in any proceedings money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability, no settlement, compromise or payment and no acceptance of money paid into Court, whenever entered into or made, is, so far as it relates to that person’s claim, valid without the approval of the Court.
@ref O 44 r 9 (src:6036)
GIVEN `money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the act is a settlement, compromise or payment, or an acceptance of money paid into Court, so far as it relates to that person's claim` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court has approved it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 44 r 9 the settlement, compromise, payment or acceptance is valid` MEANS
    BRANCH IF (NOT `money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability`) OR (NOT `the act is a settlement, compromise or payment, or an acceptance of money paid into Court, so far as it relates to that person's claim`)
           THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Where in any proceedings money is claimed by or on behalf of a person under disability, no settlement, compromise or payment and no acceptance of money paid into Court"
           IF `the Court has approved it`
           THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "without the approval of the Court" -- the bar is lifted and says nothing about validity (QA)
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is, so far as it relates to that person's claim, valid without the approval of the Court" (no approval)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Order 44 Rule 10 -- approval of settlement`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Rule 10(1): "Where, before proceedings in which a claim for money is made by or on behalf of a person under disability (whether alone or in conjunction with any other person) are begun, an agreement is reached for the
-- settlement of the claim, and it is desired to obtain the Court's approval to the settlement, then despite anything in Order 6, Rule 1(2), the claim may be made in proceedings begun by originating application and in the
-- originating application an application may also be made for (a) the approval of the Court to the settlement and such orders or directions as may be necessary to give effect to it or as may be necessary or expedient under
-- Rule 11; or (b) alternatively, directions as to the further prosecution of the claim."
